superficial beauty, huh?

00:06:26.420 --> 00:06:27.880
"SABOTAGE"

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:31.760
So, how was it, Dojima?

00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:34.720
Were you able to
convince your strict parents?

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:36.770
I convinced them, or rather...

00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:39.520
Well, they did give me more time.

00:06:40.940 --> 00:06:44.230
I see, I see. Dojima, you're a good girl.

00:06:44.230 --> 00:06:45.940
What do you mean, good girl?

00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:50.280
Well, if it were me,
I'd never keep a promise like that.

00:06:50.780 --> 00:06:54.790
That's right. It's pretty recent,
but my parents got divorced.

00:06:55.620 --> 00:06:57.450
And when they did,

00:06:57.450 --> 00:07:02.250
tons of people told them they felt sorry
for the child because of the divorce.

00:07:02.250 --> 00:07:04.670
That's a line people think of

00:07:04.670 --> 00:07:07.920
because they look down upon
children of single parents, right?

00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:11.720
I get it if they said the child gets
inconvenienced from a divorce.

00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:14.720
Anyway, when they were trying
to make such an important decision,

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:18.020
I didn't want them to use me
as some excuse.

00:07:18.020 --> 00:07:22.520
So Dojima, if you're going to give up your
dream, you have to give it up yourself,

00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:24.690
or I'd feel sorry for your Mom and Dad.

00:07:24.690 --> 00:07:27.570
Oh. Not, "I'd feel sorry"
but, "it's an inconvenience," okay?

00:07:29.950 --> 00:07:32.490
I just don't get it to begin with.

00:07:32.490 --> 00:07:38.330
Why are your Mom and Dad against
your dream of becoming an astronaut?

00:07:38.330 --> 00:07:39.870
Can it be...

00:07:39.870 --> 00:07:42.000
...that it has something to do

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:45.710
with how you get angry
when someone talks about your eyes?

00:07:48.010 --> 00:07:50.470
That has nothing to do with it.

00:07:52.380 --> 00:07:53.260
Not at all.

00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:54.220
"JOINING UP"

00:07:54.220 --> 00:07:55.850
"JOINING UP"

00:07:54.220 --> 00:07:55.850
This and that aren't related.

00:07:55.850 --> 00:07:56.760
This and that aren't related.

00:07:57.850 --> 00:07:59.220
Are you okay?

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:00.680
Perfect.

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:04.230
What part of a middle school kid
getting chased by adults is "perfect"?

00:08:04.230 --> 00:08:08.530
I-I'm all right. I mean,
Hyota nonchalantly saved me.

00:08:08.530 --> 00:08:10.240
No, that was pretty close.

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:18.490
Please brew me some tea.

00:08:18.490 --> 00:08:21.160
You've gotten rather shameless.

00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:22.500
It's fine, though.

00:08:22.500 --> 00:08:24.000
Anyone else want a drink?

00:08:25.540 --> 00:08:27.750
That's right, I don't see signs
of their leader.

00:08:28.880 --> 00:08:33.470
Oh, our leader, unlike us
members, is a Kogoro.

00:08:33.470 --> 00:08:35.050
He'll show up after school.

00:08:35.050 --> 00:08:36.050
"KOGORO"

00:08:35.050 --> 00:08:36.050
Kogoro, you said?

00:08:36.050 --> 00:08:37.260
"KOGORO AKECHI"

00:08:36.050 --> 00:08:37.260
Kogoro, you said?

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:41.270
Yes. So please bare with us
until after school.

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:47.980
I gave account of this morning's events
once again to everyone.

00:08:47.980 --> 00:08:49.690
I see.

00:08:49.690 --> 00:08:54.530
It seems the bad hunch that
I didn't want to be true has come true.

00:08:54.530 --> 00:08:58.870
Nagahiro. You knew from the beginning
that this would happen?

00:08:58.870 --> 00:09:03.250
I wasn't expecting for things to happen
this quickly, though.

00:09:03.250 --> 00:09:07.330
I didn't deem this to be dangerous
until after I started the investigation.

00:09:07.330 --> 00:09:11.210
As I was researching occurrences
ten years ago with Sosaku,

00:09:11.210 --> 00:09:14.260
a shocking truth came to light.

00:09:14.260 --> 00:09:16.760
A shocking truth?

00:09:16.760 --> 00:09:22.060
Yes. I would like to give an explanation
regarding that matter.

00:09:22.060 --> 00:09:23.480
Please listen.

00:09:23.480 --> 00:09:25.440
"VICE-CAPTAIN'S SOLUTION
FOR THE MYSTERY"

00:09:25.810 --> 00:09:30.190
Ladies and gentlemen,
I request your attention!

00:09:30.190 --> 00:09:35.280
As our leader had mentioned,
this is quite a beautiful request.

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:39.280
As a boy myself, I cannot help but feel
a spark of joy in my heart.

00:09:39.280 --> 00:09:43.000
To find a star all on your own,
and to even name it.

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:46.290
A star just for you, and no one else.

00:09:46.290 --> 00:09:48.580
And if you could name it
after your loved one,

00:09:48.580 --> 00:09:51.550
there can be no other desire
so cherished.

00:09:51.550 --> 00:09:55.130
No, this has nothing to do
with my first-grader girlfriend.

00:09:55.130 --> 00:09:57.380
Please stay quiet, Hyota.

00:09:57.380 --> 00:09:59.260
In other words,
what I want to say is that

00:09:59.260 --> 00:10:01.930
the beautiful request
of searching for a star

00:10:01.930 --> 00:10:07.480
has always been
an eternal desire from humanity.

00:10:07.480 --> 00:10:11.020
The universe is under constant watch.
It is always observed.

00:10:11.020 --> 00:10:12.230
Despite its observation,

00:10:12.230 --> 00:10:15.400
this star in question has not been
found for the past ten years.

00:10:15.400 --> 00:10:17.610
What does this mean?

00:10:17.610 --> 00:10:21.330
Yes Michiru, if we were to make
an ordinary deduction,

00:10:21.330 --> 00:10:23.910
we would say the star
never existed to begin with,

00:10:23.910 --> 00:10:26.620
that it was simply a misunderstanding
on the part of

00:10:26.620 --> 00:10:28.330
the four-year-old Miss Dojima.

00:10:29.460 --> 00:10:31.460
However, if our leader were to be present,

00:10:31.460 --> 00:10:34.170
he would rightfully reject
such a deduction,

00:10:34.170 --> 00:10:36.630
and say that it is not beautiful at all.

00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:41.970
I just mentioned that
"the universe is under constant watch."

00:10:41.970 --> 00:10:44.680
But there is a fallacy in this expression.

00:10:44.680 --> 00:10:47.390
The hint lies in the comment
made by our leader this morning.

00:10:48.310 --> 00:10:53.110
That's right. People make astronomical
observations mainly at night.

00:10:53.110 --> 00:10:56.690
Therefore, the universe is being
astronomically observed only

00:10:56.690 --> 00:10:59.660
"half the day," and not "constantly."

00:10:59.660 --> 00:11:03.410
In other words, the theory
that I had come up with earlier

00:11:03.410 --> 00:11:06.330
was that Miss Dojima didn't see the star
during the evening hours,

00:11:06.330 --> 00:11:10.210
but it was perhaps
an occurrence in broad daylight.

00:11:10.210 --> 00:11:11.830
"ASTRONOMICAL OBSERVATION (DAYTIME)"

00:11:11.830 --> 00:11:13.840
An occurrence in broad daylight?

00:11:13.840 --> 00:11:16.000
Right. That's right.

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:18.920
I was a four-year-old child, after all.

00:11:18.920 --> 00:11:21.720
It's an age that's terrible
for doing things at night.

00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:23.510
But what of it?

00:11:23.510 --> 00:11:27.520
Even if it was daytime in Japan,
but night in some foreign country,

00:11:27.520 --> 00:11:31.310
wouldn't the skies
be destined to be observed after all?

00:11:31.310 --> 00:11:33.060
However, that depends

00:11:33.060 --> 00:11:35.440
on where you deem the universe to be.

00:11:36.610 --> 00:11:37.940
To put it simply,

00:11:37.940 --> 00:11:39.820
if the star that Miss Dojima
claims to have seen

00:11:39.820 --> 00:11:42.950
was at a distance
extremely close to Earth,

00:11:42.950 --> 00:11:45.370
you could say that it would be
impossible to observe it

00:11:45.370 --> 00:11:47.830
outside Japan based on its angle.

00:11:47.830 --> 00:11:50.620
Also, similar to how you can see
the moon during the day,

00:11:50.620 --> 00:11:55.500
you could say that she was able to witness
it during the day because it was close by.

00:11:55.500 --> 00:11:57.130
I see.

00:11:57.130 --> 00:12:01.840
You know what? It's like how
you can see meteors during the day.

00:12:01.840 --> 00:12:04.470
Does that mean she saw a meteor
burning up in the atmosphere

00:12:04.470 --> 00:12:06.850
and mistook it for a star?

00:12:06.850 --> 00:12:10.060
However, if it's an object
flying in from outer space,

00:12:10.060 --> 00:12:11.850
even if it were landing in Japan,

00:12:11.850 --> 00:12:15.230
it would still be witnessed
all over the world.

00:12:16.270 --> 00:12:21.110
However, on the point that it
"burned up" is basically correct.

00:12:21.110 --> 00:12:23.110
Sakiguchi?

00:12:23.110 --> 00:12:24.870
I appreciate your thoughtfulness.

00:12:26.200 --> 00:12:30.700
So, it's okay for you
to be blunt about it.

00:12:30.700 --> 00:12:32.410
I'm ready to accept it now.

00:12:33.870 --> 00:12:36.040
If an object that was
hardly at an altitude

00:12:36.040 --> 00:12:38.630
that you could recognize
as being in outer space,

00:12:38.630 --> 00:12:42.590
and that was the star
that I had witnessed,

00:12:42.590 --> 00:12:45.640
then that is no longer
what people would call a new star.

00:12:46.640 --> 00:12:48.310
That means it wasn't a star that would be

00:12:48.310 --> 00:12:49.520
"MAYUMI DOJIMA"
"ASTRONAUT"

00:12:49.520 --> 00:12:51.180
"MAYUMI DOJIMA"
"ASTRONAUT"

00:12:49.520 --> 00:12:51.180
the objective of a dream where
one would wish to depart for it.

00:12:51.180 --> 00:12:54.560
the objective of a dream where
one would wish to depart for it.

00:12:54.560 --> 00:12:56.860
I made a mistake of some kind.

00:12:56.860 --> 00:13:03.320
I'm sure I made a mistake somewhere,
and completely wasted the last ten years.

00:13:03.320 --> 00:13:06.700
Therefore, no matter what kind of
conclusion is reached,

00:13:06.700 --> 00:13:09.160
I won't blame it on Sakiguchi.

00:13:10.450 --> 00:13:12.910
I won't blame
the Pretty Boy Detective Club.

00:13:13.870 --> 00:13:16.750
No matter what the conclusion,
or no matter what the deduction.

00:13:16.750 --> 00:13:19.420
I will accept it as my responsibility
since I was the one who's been

00:13:19.420 --> 00:13:20.630
making that mistake.

00:13:22.630 --> 00:13:26.300
Please say it. Please tell me.

00:13:26.300 --> 00:13:29.930
What was that object I saw that day?

00:13:33.560 --> 00:13:36.440
It wasn't just any star,
but an artificial satellite.

00:13:38.810 --> 00:13:41.190
It wasn't just any artificial satellite...

00:13:43.110 --> 00:13:45.070
"SOSAKU YUBIWA"

00:13:43.110 --> 00:13:45.070
...but a military satellite.

00:13:45.070 --> 00:13:46.570
"SPACE WAR"

00:13:47.160 --> 00:13:50.030
Imagine a certain civilian
military company

00:13:50.030 --> 00:13:55.160
developed and launched an independent
military satellite in secret.

00:13:55.160 --> 00:13:58.580
Along with, of course,
careful consideration of camouflaging.

00:13:58.580 --> 00:14:02.630
They fabricated some other
objective and launched it.

00:14:02.630 --> 00:14:05.010
But the mission had to be aborted.

00:14:05.010 --> 00:14:06.300
Aborted?

00:14:06.300 --> 00:14:08.930
By that, I mean it was shot down.

00:14:08.930 --> 00:14:13.600
It had been shot down, and it was
an event with no public knowledge.

00:14:14.560 --> 00:14:17.140
Then, the star that I had seen...

00:14:17.140 --> 00:14:22.480
In other words, it was the light
from when that satellite was shot down?

00:14:22.480 --> 00:14:24.440
That's probably what happened.

00:14:24.440 --> 00:14:27.530
Huh? But isn't that strange?
We're talking about outer space.

00:14:27.530 --> 00:14:30.530
There's no oxygen out there.
We learned that in grade school.

00:14:30.530 --> 00:14:34.120
Without oxygen, matter won't combust.

00:14:35.200 --> 00:14:38.790
I see, you don't learn about that
in grade school.

00:14:38.790 --> 00:14:41.920
Even without oxygen, even in outer space,

00:14:41.920 --> 00:14:43.960
there's an exceptional form
of combustion that happens

00:14:43.960 --> 00:14:46.130
regardless of trivial matters.

00:14:46.970 --> 00:14:49.130
Nuclear fusion.

00:14:49.130 --> 00:14:50.760
Shot down by a nuke.

00:14:50.760 --> 00:14:53.560
That's something
that can't be public knowledge.

00:14:53.560 --> 00:14:55.890
And as if nothing had happened,

00:14:55.890 --> 00:15:00.560
